-- title:
--   Flatness of the πⁿ⁺¹-truncation of a flat algebra
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a commutative ring, $\pi \in \mathcal O$ an element, and $S$ a commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra which is flat as an $\mathcal O$-module; let $n$ be a natural number. Consider the ideal $(\pi^{n+1}) \subseteq \mathcal O$ generated by $\pi^{n+1}$ and the ideal of $S$ generated by the single element $(\mathrm{algebraMap}\ \pi)^{n+1}$, i.e. the image of $\pi^{n+1}$ under the structure map; the structure map carries the first ideal into the contraction of the second, so it induces a ring homomorphism $\mathcal O/(\pi^{n+1}) \to S/\pi^{n+1}S$ via `Ideal.quotientMap`. The assertion is that the morphism of affine schemes obtained by applying $\operatorname{Spec}$ to this induced homomorphism, that is
--   $$\operatorname{Spec}\bigl(S/\pi^{n+1}S\bigr) \longrightarrow \operatorname{Spec}\bigl(\mathcal O/(\pi^{n+1})\bigr),$$
--   satisfies the morphism property `AlgebraicGeometry.Flat`, i.e. is a flat morphism of schemes. No hypotheses beyond flatness of $S$ over $\mathcal O$ are imposed: $\pi$ is an arbitrary element and $\mathcal O$ an arbitrary commutative ring.
--
--   This is the statement that the truncations of a flat algebra stay flat: passing from $\mathcal O \to S$ to $\mathcal O/(\pi^{n+1}) \to S/\pi^{n+1}S$ preserves flatness, phrased for the morphism of affine schemes. It is used in the treatment of towers of truncated bases, where it makes the comparison morphisms of a base change of a `TowerQuotientDatum` flat, in [`AlgebraicGeometry.TowerQuotientDatum.exists_baseChange_of_flat_of_isPullback`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.TowerQuotientDatum.exists_baseChange_of_flat_of_isPullback) and in [`AlgebraicGeometry.TowerQuotientDatum.univ_loc_of_isPullback_of_flat_of_forall_exists_chart`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.TowerQuotientDatum.univ_loc_of_isPullback_of_flat_of_forall_exists_chart).
